  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAn upper low centered just to our north will slowly move eastward \ntonight and become nearly stationary in the vicinity of Nova Scotia \nthrough Saturday. Cyclonic flow and shortwaves stemming from this \nlow will bring scattered showers and isolated storms during the \nfirst part of this weekend, mainly during the daytime hours. The \nbetter chances today will be around the mountains and western ME and \nthen a few may linger in the mountains through tonight. Not \nexpecting as much in the way of fog overnight into early Saturday as \nwinds may stay up just enough, but I do have patchy fog in the \nforecast along the Connecticut, Pemi, and Saco River valleys where \nwinds do look light enough. \n\nOn Saturday, clouds will increase in the morning and afternoon \nwith the better chances for showers across the mountains and NH\nthis time around as a backdoor front moves through the area. \nTemperatures come down a few degrees with most in the low-mid \n70s, but some locations that stay cloudier could remain in the \n60s. It will also be somewhat breezy, especially across western \nME, where gusts will be out of the north around 20 mph at times.\n\nAnother wave drops south into northern New England Saturday night, \nwhich could bring a few more showers to NH and the mountains. Due to \nthis chance, valley fog has been left out of the forecast for \nSaturday night. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nHigh pressure will arrive from the west come Sunday morning. \nPredominantly northwest winds linger for the day, remaining \nbreezy due to the tight pressure gradient just east. Despite the\ndecreasing clouds, temperatures will be similar to Saturday, in\nthe low to mid 70s.\n\nOvernight, the high may never make it directly overhead. This\nkeeps some gradient wind over the northern and eastern CWA.\nWhile skies clear, this could keep the night from being more\nefficient for cooling. Spots that do stand to drop a few extra\ndegrees would be to the south and west. This could let lows in\nthe mid 40s trickle into parts of southern NH. \n\nHigh pressure over the Great Lakes Monday will provide another\npleasant day with dry weather. Temps tick a few degrees warmer\nas the source region is from the west, the same for Tuesday.\nHowever, a weak cold front will be moving through northern New\nEngland. There could be a few showers that accompany passage,\nbut not enough to help with ongoing dry conditions.\n\nGuidance continues to depict a larger system moving into the\nregion in the mid to late week timeframe. While ensembles depict\na likely probability of greater than 500 kg/m/s of IVT, it is\nprogressive and quickly translates the forecast area. This seems\nto mirror a lot of our systems this summer, and may be just\nenough to deliver a wetting rain, too little to make a larger \ndent in rainfall deficits.",
